---
import Breadcrumb from "../components/Breadcrumb.astro";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  CENTIMETERS_PER_INCH,
  CUBIC_FEET_PER_CUBIC_YARD,
  gravelPresets,
  kilogramsPerCubicMeter,
  KILOGRAMS_PER_POUND,
  METERS_PER_FOOT,
  mulchPresets,
  POUNDS_PER_SHORT_TON,
  soilPresets,
  SQUARE_FEET_PER_CUBIC_YARD_AT_ONE_INCH,
  tonsPerCubicYard,
  type DensityPreset,
} from "../lib/calc";
import { formatNumber } from "../lib/calc";
import { site } from "../config/site";
import { webPageJsonLd } from "../lib/seo";

const title = "Methodology — Formulas and Densities | Yardwork";
const description =
  "Formulas, unit conversions, and bulk densities behind the Yardwork gravel, mulch, topsoil, and sod calculators, with the reference for each figure.";
const pathname = "/methodology/";

const groups: { heading: string; presets: readonly DensityPreset[] }[] = [
  { heading: "Gravel and stone", presets: gravelPresets },
  { heading: "Mulch", presets: mulchPresets },
  { heading: "Soil", presets: soilPresets },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={webPageJsonLd(title, description, pathname)}>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <Breadcrumb current="Methodology" />
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The calculators share one module. These are the formulas, the rounding rules, and the
      densities, including where each density comes from and where it is only a planning midpoint.
    </p>
  </header>
  <article class="prose">
    <p>Last updated {site.updatedLabel}.</p>

    <h2>Area</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Rectangle: length × width.</li>
      <li>Circle: π × (diameter ÷ 2)². The field asks for the diameter because that is the distance across.</li>
      <li>Triangle: ½ × base × height, with height measured square to the base.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      US lengths are in feet. Metric lengths are in meters and are converted with the international
      foot, exactly {METERS_PER_FOOT} meters. One square meter is
      {formatNumber(1 / METERS_PER_FOOT ** 2, 4)} square feet.
    </p>

    <h2>Volume</h2>
    <p>
      Depth in inches is divided by 12 to get feet. Depth in centimeters is divided by 100 to get
      meters. Volume is area times that depth.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>1 cubic yard = {CUBIC_FEET_PER_CUBIC_YARD} cubic feet.</li>
      <li>1 inch of depth over 1 square foot is 1/12 cubic foot.</li>
      <li>
        One cubic yard spread 1 inch deep covers {SQUARE_FEET_PER_CUBIC_YARD_AT_ONE_INCH} square
        feet, because 27 × 12 = 324. Coverage at any depth is 324 ÷ depth in inches.
      </li>
      <li>1 inch = {CENTIMETERS_PER_INCH} centimeters, exactly.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Cubic meters are converted from cubic feet with the cube of {METERS_PER_FOOT}. The metric
      toggle changes the fields you type into. Yards, short tons, and US bag sizes stay in the
      result because that is how the material is sold here.
    </p>

    <h2>Allowance</h2>
    <p>
      The allowance is a percent added to the net volume or area. Ten percent multiplies the result
      by 1.10. It is capped at 100 percent. Zero is allowed.
    </p>
    <p>
      For decorative gravel and for mulch, treat it as waste: spillage, odd edges, and a thin spot.
      For crusher run, decomposed granite, and other base that will be compacted, the finished
      thickness is less than the loose thickness, so the allowance is doing more work. It is not a
      Proctor compaction test. If a specification calls for a compacted depth, ask how much loose
      material that depth requires.
    </p>
    <p>
      Soil uses the same multiplier for settling after watering. Sod uses it for pieces cut off
      along edges and curves. Mulch does not need a compaction factor.
    </p>

    <h2>Weight</h2>
    <p>
      Short tons = cubic feet × pounds per cubic foot ÷ {POUNDS_PER_SHORT_TON.toLocaleString("en-US")}.
      A short ton is 2,000 pounds. A metric tonne is 1,000 kilograms. The pound used in the
      conversion is the international avoirdupois pound, exactly {KILOGRAMS_PER_POUND} kilograms.
      Metric tonnes in the result are that conversion of the pound weight, not a second density.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tons per cubic yard = pounds per cubic foot × 27 ÷ 2,000. At 100 pounds per cubic foot, a
      cubic yard is exactly 1.35 short tons. At about 74 pounds per cubic foot, a cubic yard and a
      short ton are the same weight. Ordinary gravel and topsoil are heavier than that, which is
      why a ton and a yard are different orders.
    </p>

    <h2>Bags, pallets, and money</h2>
    <p>
      A volume bag count is ordered cubic feet divided by the bag’s cubic feet. A weight bag count
      is ordered pounds divided by the bag’s pounds. Pallet and roll counts are ordered square feet
      divided by the size you enter. Anything that is not already a whole number rounds up to the
      next whole bag, pallet, or roll, because that is what you have to buy. Exact values are shown
      in the working so you can see how much the rounding added.
    </p>
    <p>
      A price per cubic yard is multiplied by ordered yards. A price per short ton is multiplied by
      ordered tons. A price per bag is multiplied by the rounded-up bag count. A sod price per
      square foot uses the order area, and a price per pallet uses whole pallets. Each price is a
      separate estimate. Delivery, tax, fabric, edging, and labor are not included. Displayed money
      is rounded to the cent.
    </p>

    <h2>Densities</h2>
    <p>
      Bulk density changes with moisture, particle size, and how tightly the load is packed. The
      presets are loose planning figures. They are not a lab report for the pile on a particular
      truck. The Engineering Toolbox table for
      <a href="https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/dirt-mud-densities-d_1727.html">dirt and mud densities</a>
      lists gravel at 105 pounds per cubic foot dry and 125 wet, sand at 97 dry and 119 wet, loam
      at 80, loose dry dirt at 76, and loose moist dirt at 78. Its
      <a href="https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/density-materials-d_1652.html">common-products table</a>
      lists bark and wood refuse at 10 to 20 pounds per cubic foot. Kilograms per cubic meter on
      this site are converted from the pound figure, so they can differ by a rounding step from a
      table that prints both units.
    </p>
    {
      groups.map((group) => (
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>{group.heading}</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Material</th>
                <th class="num">lb/cu ft</th>
                <th class="num">tons/cu yd</th>
                <th class="num">kg/cu m</th>
                <th>Published range</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {group.presets.map((preset) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    {preset.name}
                    <br />
                    <span class="field-help">{preset.basis}</span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="num">{formatNumber(preset.poundsPerCubicFoot, 0)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{formatNumber(tonsPerCubicYard(preset.poundsPerCubicFoot), 2)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{formatNumber(kilogramsPerCubicMeter(preset.poundsPerCubicFoot), 0)}</td>
                  <td>{preset.publishedRange}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      ))
    }
    <p>
      Pea gravel, river rock, crusher run, decomposed granite, lava rock, and the mulch weights are
      planning midpoints of ranges suppliers and reference tables commonly quote. They are labeled
      that way in the basis column. Crushed stone uses the Engineering Toolbox dry-gravel figure
      directly. Screened topsoil uses the loam figure. Fill dirt uses loose dry dirt. When a
      supplier has tested a product, the test often follows a loose bulk-density method such as
      ASTM C29. This site has not run those tests.
    </p>

    <h2>Mulch depth</h2>
    <p>
      The depth guidance on the mulch calculator follows extension publications, not a density
      table.
      <a href="https://extension.psu.edu/mulching-landscape-trees">Penn State Extension</a>
      recommends 2 to 4 inches of organic mulch, less on poorly drained soil and with fine
      shredded mulch, kept off the root flare.
      <a href="https://yardandgarden.extension.iastate.edu/faq/how-deep-should-mulch-layer-be-around-trees-and-shrubs">Iowa State University Extension</a>
      suggests 3 to 4 inches of wood chips or shredded bark on well-drained sites and 2 to 3 inches
      on heavy soil, kept at least 6 inches off the trunk.
      <a href="https://extension.illinois.edu/sites/default/files/proper_mulching_techniques_infosheet.pdf">University of Illinois Extension</a>
      and
      <a href="https://hort.extension.wisc.edu/articles/wood-mulch-and-tree-health/">Wisconsin Horticulture</a>
      give the same 2 to 4 inch band and the same warning against mulch piled on the bark.
    </p>

    <h2>Sod sizes</h2>
    <p>
      A pallet of about 450 square feet, in rolls of about 10 square feet, is a common US sales
      unit. It is not a standard you can look up the way you can look up the foot. Farms also sell
      400 and 500 square foot pallets and smaller slabs. Both sizes are fields in the calculator
      for that reason. The default 5 percent waste allowance is a planning figure for ordinary edge
      cuts on a rectangle. Curved layouts waste more.
    </p>

    <h2>Rounding and what the tools will not do</h2>
    <p>
      Displayed areas, yards, and short tons are rounded for reading. The bag, pallet, and roll
      counts use the full value and then round up. Two calculators that you add together by hand
      should be added from the cubic yards or square feet, not from already-rounded bag counts.
    </p>
    <p>
      These tools estimate material for an order. They do not design a driveway section, a drainage
      system, a retaining wall, or a soil specification. They do not know how wet tomorrow’s load
      will be. For a structural base, a drainage trench, or a large grade change, use the
      contractor’s or engineer’s depth and the supplier’s density, and treat this page as the
      arithmetic.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
